     1                                  ; ==========================================================================
     2                                  ; Itsy 16-bit DOS COM Forth - Introduction / Overview
     3                                  ; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
     4                                  ; Itsy is a classical, indirect-threaded Forth interpreter for 16-bit DOS.
     5                                  ; It implements a single flat memory model where code and data coexist. The
     6                                  ; system is centered on a dictionary of linked entries, each holding a name,
     7                                  ; flags, a code field, and optional data. Execution is driven by two loops:
     8                                  ;   - Outer interpreter: parses input, searches the dictionary, and manages
     9                                  ;     compilation versus immediate execution.
    10                                  ;   - Inner interpreter: dispatches words by fetching their code field
    11                                  ;     addresses and jumping indirectly, forming the core of the threading
    12                                  ;     model.
    13                                  ;
    14                                  ; The interpreter is fully self-extending: new words can be defined at
    15                                  ; runtime, primitives and assembly routines coexist seamlessly, and the
    16                                  ; dictionary serves as both program and environment. Register assignments
    17                                  ; and small helper routines support efficient stack handling, colon
    18                                  ; definitions, and dictionary access. This overview frames the architecture
    19                                  ; detailed below.
    20                                  ; ==========================================================================
    21                                  
    22                                  ; ==========================================================================
    23                                  ; Itsy 16-bit DOS COM Forth - Architecture, Dictionary, and Core Routines
    24                                  ; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
    25                                  ; -----------------------
    26                                  ; Dictionary Structure
    27                                  ; -----------------------
    28                                  ; The dictionary is a linked list of headers, each containing a name,
    29                                  ; flags, a code field, and a data field. Threaded code is stored as a
    30                                  ; sequence of addresses pointing to the code fields of previously defined
    31                                  ; words. In Itsy, SI is the instruction pointer, fetching each cell that
    32                                  ; points to a dictionary code field.
    33                                  
    34                                  ; -----------------------
    35                                  ; Dictionary Words
    36                                  ; -----------------------
    37                                  ; Each entry in the dictionary represents a Forth word: a variable,
    38                                  ; constant, primitive, or colon definition. Words are self-describing
    39                                  ; and contain four main fields:
    40                                  ;   LFA - pointer to the previous dictionary entry
    41                                  ;   NFA - pointer to the word’s name string
    42                                  ;   CFA - pointer to executable code (primitive or colon handler)
    43                                  ;   DFA - pointer to associated data or value (if applicable)
    44                                  ;
    45                                  ; Colon definitions store a sequence of CFA addresses forming threaded
    46                                  ; code. Primitives, variables, and constants each have short handlers
    47                                  ; that execute their behavior and then return to the inner interpreter.
    48                                  ; This structure allows traversal, execution, and runtime modification
    49                                  ; of the dictionary.
    50                                  
    51                                  ; -----------------------
    52                                  ; Implemented Dictionary Words
    53                                  ; -----------------------
    54                                  ;   state   >in       #tib      dp        base     last
    55                                  ;   tib     abort     ,         lit       rot      drop
    56                                  ;   dup     swap      +         =         @        !
    57                                  ;   0branch branch    execute   exit      count    >number
    58                                  ;   accept  word       emit      find       :        ;
    59                                  ;   (;code) constant  interpret
    60                                  
    61                                  ; -----------------------
    62                                  ; Inner Interpreter
    63                                  ; -----------------------
    64                                  ; Execution passes through a single inner interpreter loop labeled `next`.
    65                                  ; This loop uses LODSW at SI to fetch the next code field, moves it to DI,
    66                                  ; and jumps indirectly via `jmp word[di]`. All words, primitive or colon-
    67                                  ; defined, are dispatched through this loop, defining the indirect threading
    68                                  ; model.
    69                                  
    70                                  ; -----------------------
    71                                  ; Register Usage
    72                                  ; -----------------------
    73                                  ;   SI  - instruction pointer
    74                                  ;   DI  - current code field pointer
    75                                  ;   BX  - top of parameter stack
    76                                  ;   SP  - hardware stack for remaining stack items
    77                                  ;   BP  - software return stack pointer for colon definitions
    78                                  
    79                                  ; -----------------------
    80                                  ; Colon Definitions
    81                                  ; -----------------------
    82                                  ; Colon definitions invoke `docolon`, which pushes the old SI onto BP,
    83                                  ; sets SI to the new definition’s threaded code, and jumps to `next`.
    84                                  ; `exit` restores SI from BP to resume the caller’s execution.
    85                                  
    86                                  ; -----------------------
    87                                  ; Code Field Routines
    88                                  ; -----------------------
    89                                  ; Each dictionary entry’s code field points to a short routine:
    90                                  ;   - Primitives perform a single machine operation and jump to `next`.
    91                                  ;   - Colon definitions invoke `docolon`.
    92                                  ;   - Variables and constants use `dovar` and `doconst` to push addresses
    93                                  ;     or values before returning to `next`.
    94                                  
    95                                  ; -----------------------
    96                                  ; Outer Interpreter
    97                                  ; -----------------------
    98                                  ; The outer interpreter reads input, tokenizes it with `word`, searches
    99                                  ; the dictionary via `find`, and either executes or compiles based on
   100                                  ; the current state stored in `state`. Memory is a single flat segment:
   101                                  ; the dictionary grows upward, stacks grow downward, matching the COM file
   102                                  ; model.
   103                                  
   104                                  ; -----------------------
   105                                  ; Memory Layout & Self-Extending Behavior
   106                                  ; -----------------------
   107                                  ; Definitions, variables, and primitives exist alongside executable code.
   108                                  ; New Forth words can execute additional assembly instructions. Program
   109                                  ; flow is closely tied to memory layout, initialization, and runtime code
   110                                  ; generation. The dictionary functions as both program and environment.
   111                                  
   112                                  ; -----------------------
   113                                  ; Architectural Assumptions
   114                                  ; -----------------------
   115                                  ; Indirect threading is used; threaded code holds code field addresses, not
   116                                  ; machine code; one shared interpreter loop exists; parameter and return
   117                                  ; stacks are distinct; memory is flat. These assumptions follow early
   118                                  ; assembly-based Forths and are reflected in Itsy’s design.
   119                                  
   120                                  ; -----------------------
   121                                  ; Core Helper Routines
   122                                  ; -----------------------
   123                                  ;   getchar   - read a character from input
   124                                  ;   outchar   - write a character to output
   125                                  ;   docolon   - execute a colon-defined word
   126                                  ;   dovar     - handle variable access
   127                                  ;   doconst   - handle constant access
   128                                  ;   next      - inner interpreter loop
   129                                  
   130                                  ;
   131                                  ; These components together form the complete Itsy Forth system.
   132                                  ; ==========================================================================
   133                                  
   134                                  ; -------------------------------------
   135                                  ; -- Program Entry Point
   136                                  ; -------------------------------------
   137                                  
   138                                  ; -----------------------
   139                                  ; Assembly Note
   140                                  ; -----------------------
   141                                  ; Sets the program entry point at 0100h for a DOS COM file.
   142                                  ; Immediately jumps to the code field of the 'abort' routine (CFA).
   143                                  ; This jump also serves to initialize the interpreter and memory state
   144                                  ; before execution enters the main inner interpreter loop.
   145                                  
   146                                  ; -----------------------
   147                                  ; Forth Note
   148                                  ; -----------------------
   149                                  ; At Forth level, this corresponds to performing any initial setup and
   150                                  ; clearing the interpreter state. The 'abort' word is used to reset
   151                                  ; or terminate execution safely, ensuring the system starts in a
   152                                  ; known, consistent state.
   153                                  
   154                                  org 0100h
   155 00000000 EB54                    jmp cfa_abort+2     ; jump to abort code start (initialize the program)
   156                                  
   157                                  
   158                                  ; -------------------------------------
   159                                  ; -- Forth Variables: all Forth words
   160                                  ; -------------------------------------
   161                                  
   162                                  ; -----------------------
   163                                  ; Assembly Note
   164                                  ; -----------------------
   165                                  ; This section sets up Forth variables as dictionary entries.
   166                                  ; Each variable allocates memory for LFA, NFA, CFA, and DFA fields.
   167                                  ; The assembler initializes links (LFA), names (NFA), and default values
   168                                  ; (DFA). DP points to free memory for the next entry. BX is used as a
   169                                  ; temporary pointer during initialization.
   170                                  
   171                                  ; -----------------------
   172                                  ; Forth Note
   173                                  ; -----------------------
   174                                  ; These entries correspond to Forth variables referenced at runtime:
   175                                  ;   state   >in   #tib   dp   base   last
   176                                  ; They can be fetched or stored using Forth primitives (FETCH, STORE),
   177                                  ; manipulated on the parameter stack, and accessed in colon definitions.
   178                                  
   179                                                                          ; state
   180 00000002 0000                    lfa_state:          dw 0
   181 00000004 057374617465            nfa_state:          db 5,'state'
   182 0000000A [FC02]                  cfa_state:          dw dovar
   183 0000000C 0000                    dfa_state:          dw 0
   184                                  
   185                                                                          ; to_in [>in]
   186 0000000E [0200]                  lfa_to_in:          dw lfa_state
   187 00000010 033E696E                nfa_to_in:          db 3,'>in'
   188 00000014 [FC02]                  cfa_to_in:          dw  dovar
   189 00000016 0000                    dfa_to_in:          dw 0
   190                                  
   191                                                                          ; ntib [#tib]
   192 00000018 [0E00]                  lfa_ntib:           dw lfa_to_in
   193 0000001A 0423746962              nfa_ntib:           db 4,'#tib'
   194 0000001F [FC02]                  cfa_ntib:           dw  dovar
   195 00000021 0000                    dfa_ntib:           dw 0
   196                                  
   197                                                                          ; dp
   198 00000023 [1800]                  lfa_dp:             dw lfa_ntib
   199 00000025 026470                  nfa_dp:             db 2,'dp'
   200 00000028 [FC02]                  cfa_dp:             dw  dovar
   201 0000002A [DC03]                  dfa_dp:             dw freemem
   202                                  
   203                                                                          ; base
   204 0000002C [2300]                  lfa_base:           dw lfa_dp
   205 0000002E 0462617365              nfa_base:           db 4,'base'
   206 00000033 [FC02]                  cfa_base:           dw  dovar
   207 00000035 0A00                    dfa_base:           dw 10
   208                                  
   209                                                                          ; last
   210 00000037 [2C00]                  lfa_last:           dw lfa_base
   211 00000039 046C617374              nfa_last:           db 4,'last'
   212 0000003E [FC02]                  cfa_last:           dw  dovar
   213 00000040 [D203]                  dfa_last:           dw final
   214                                  
   215                                                                          ; tib
   216 00000042 [3700]                  lfa_tib:            dw lfa_last
   217 00000044 03746962                nfa_tib:            db 3,'tib'
   218 00000048 [3903]                  cfa_tib:            dw  doconst
   219 0000004A 0080                    dfa_tib:            dw 32768
   220                                  
   221                                  ; ------------------------------
   222                                  ; -- Forth Note - Dictionary Chain
   223                                  ; ------------------------------
   224                                  ; The chain so far contains the dictionary head followed by the
   225                                  ; core Forth variables:
   226                                  ;
   227                                  ; 0 <- state <- >in <- #tib <- dp <- base <- last <- tib
   228                                  ;
   229                                  ; This establishes the initial environment for the interpreter and
   230                                  ; supports later word lookups and compilation.
   231                                  ;
   232                                  ; Each entry points to the previous one, forming a linked list that
   233                                  ; supports dictionary traversal for lookup and compilation. This shows
   234                                  ; the incremental setup of the Forth environment prior to defining
   235                                  ; runtime primitives.
   236                                  
   237                                  ; -------------------------------------
   238                                  ; -- System Primitives: all Forth words
   239                                  ; -------------------------------------
   240                                  ; The following entries define foundational primitives required to initialize
   241                                  ; the Forth environment and support early runtime operations.
   242                                  
   243                                  ; -----------------------
   244                                  ; Assembly Note
   245                                  ; -----------------------
   246                                  ; This section creates the core Forth primitives in assembly. Each
   247                                  ; primitive is formed with a dictionary header (LFA, NFA, CFA). The
   248                                  ; code field (CFA) points to the assembly-language routine implementing
   249                                  ; the primitive, which is usually a jump two bytes ahead to the actual
   250                                  ; routine. The routines are straight assembly instructions and each
   251                                  ; ends with `jmp next` to return control to the inner interpreter. The
   252                                  ; data field (DFA) is unused for primitives; it is used only for
   253                                  ; variables or constants. DP tracks free memory for dictionary entries.
   254                                  
   255                                  ; -----------------------
   256                                  ; Forth Note
   257                                  ; -----------------------
   258                                  ; These primitives are foundational and somewhat special because they
   259                                  ; can be defined before the inner interpreter loop exists. While they
   260                                  ; ultimately rely on `next` for control transfer, their machine
   261                                  ; routines themselves do not depend on the inner interpreter being
   262                                  ; fully established. They include stack operations (DUP, DROP, SWAP,
   263                                  ; ROT), arithmetic (+, =), memory access (@, !), flow control
   264                                  ; (BRANCH, 0BRANCH, EXIT), I/O (EMIT, ACCEPT), and mechanisms to
   265                                  ; define new words (LIT, COLON, CONSTANT, VARIABLE). Primitives have no
   266                                  ; associated data; variables and constants populate DFA to hold their
   267                                  ; value or address.
   268                                  
   269                                  
   270                                  ; ---------
   271                                  ; -- Dictionary Word: abort
   272                                  ; -- System Initialization Primitive
   273                                  ; ---------
   274                                  
   275                                  ; -----------------------
   276                                  ; Assembly Note
   277                                  ; -----------------------
   278                                  ; This code builds the primitive `abort` entry in the dictionary. A
   279                                  ; header is formed with LFA pointing to the previous word, NFA holding
   280                                  ; the name, and CFA pointing two bytes ahead to the following assembly
   281                                  ; implementation. The routine itself initializes core interpreter
   282                                  ; state (state, >in, SP, SI) and jumps to the inner interpreter loop.
   283                                  
   284                                  ; -----------------------
   285                                  ; Forth Note
   286                                  ; -----------------------
   287                                  ; abort is a special primitive required to bootstrap the system.
   288                                  ; It sets up initial stack and interpreter state so subsequent Forth
   289                                  ; words can execute. Though it precedes the fully defined inner
   290                                  ; interpreter, it relies on next to dispatch execution of other words.
   291                                  ; abort is also callable by Forth user programs to reset the interpreter
   292                                  ; state and exit a running definition safely.
   293                                  
   294                                                                          ; abort
   295 0000004C [4200]                  lfa_abort:          dw lfa_tib
   296 0000004E 0561626F7274            nfa_abort:          db 5,'abort'
   297 00000054 [5600]                  cfa_abort:          dw $+2
   298                                  
   299 00000056 A1[2100]                                    mov ax,word[dfa_ntib]       ; load value in #tib (0 to start)
   300 00000059 A3[1600]                                    mov word[dfa_to_in],ax      ; Sets >in the value in #tib
   301 0000005C 31ED                                        xor bp,bp                   ; clear bp (used to move 0 into state)
   302 0000005E 892E[0C00]                                  mov word[dfa_state],bp      ; sets state to 0 (enter interpret mode)
   303 00000062 BC00FF                                      mov sp,-256                 ; reserves a 256-byte stack from top of segment           
   304 00000065 BE[4E03]                                    mov si,cfa_interpret+2      ; set si to interpreter code start
   305 00000068 E98400                                      jmp next                    ; jump to inner interpreter’s dispatch loop
   306                                  
   307                                  ; ---------
   308                                  ; -- Dictionary Words: comma [,] , lit
   309                                  ; -- Compilation Primitives
   310                                  ; ---------
   311                                  
   312                                  ; -----------------------
   313                                  ; Assembly Note
   314                                  ; -----------------------
   315                                  ; `comma` builds a threaded code cell in the dictionary by storing
   316                                  ; the value in AX (swapped with BX) at the current dictionary pointer.
   317                                  ; The dictionary pointer is then updated, and execution returns to
   318                                  ; the inner interpreter via `jmp next`.
   319                                  
   320                                  ; -----------------------
   321                                  ; Forth Note
   322                                  ; -----------------------
   323                                  ; `comma` (`,`) is a compilation primitive used by colon definitions
   324                                  ; to append a cell to the threaded code of the currently compiling word.
   325                                  ; It operates directly on the dictionary and does not execute code itself.
   326                                  
   327                                                                          ; comma [,]
   328 0000006B [4C00]                  lfa_comma:          dw lfa_abort
   329 0000006D 012C                    nfa_comma:          db 1,','
   330 0000006F [7100]                  cfa_comma:          dw $+2
   331                                  
   332 00000071 8B3E[2A00]                                  mov di,word[dfa_dp]
   333 00000075 93                                          xchg ax,bx
   334 00000076 AB                                          stosw
   335 00000077 893E[2A00]                                  mov word[dfa_dp],di
   336 0000007B 5B                                          pop bx
   337 0000007C EB71                                        jmp next
   338                                  
   339                                  ; -----------------------
   340                                  ; Assembly Note
   341                                  ; -----------------------
   342                                  ; `lit` fetches the next word-sized literal from the dictionary using
   343                                  ; LODSW, pushes BX to preserve the top of the parameter stack, swaps AX/BX,
   344                                  ; and returns to the inner interpreter via `jmp next`. This creates a
   345                                  ; runtime literal for subsequent execution.
   346                                  
   347                                  ; -----------------------
   348                                  ; Forth Note
   349                                  ; -----------------------
   350                                  ; `lit` is a compilation primitive that pushes a literal value onto the
   351                                  ; parameter stack when executed. It is typically invoked by colon definitions
   352                                  ; to embed constants directly in threaded code.
   353                                  
   354                                                                          ; lit
   355 0000007E [6B00]                  lfa_lit:            dw lfa_comma
   356 00000080 036C6974                nfa_lit:            db 3,'lit'
   357 00000084 [8600]                  cfa_lit:            dw $+2
   358                                  
   359 00000086 53                                          push bx
   360 00000087 AD                                          lodsw
   361 00000088 93                                          xchg ax,bx
   362 00000089 EB64                                        jmp next
   363                                  
   364                                  ; ---------
   365                                  ; -- Dictionary Words: rot, drop, dup, swap
   366                                  ; -- Stack Primitives
   367                                  ; ---------
   368                                  
   369                                  ; -----------------------
   370                                  ; -- Assembly Note - stack primitives
   371                                  ; -----------------------
   372                                  ; Each primitive is implemented as a short assembly routine.
   373                                  ; `rot` pops two items off the parameter stack (BX/SP), rearranges
   374                                  ; them, and pushes them back in rotated order. `drop` pops the top
   375                                  ; item. `dup` pushes a copy of the top item. `swap` exchanges the
   376                                  ; top two items. Each routine ends with `jmp next` to return to the
   377                                  ; inner interpreter loop.
   378                                  
   379                                  ; -----------------------
   380                                  ; -- Forth Note - stack primitives
   381                                  ; -----------------------
   382                                  ; These stack primitives manipulate the parameter stack directly.
   383                                  ; They are fundamental to word execution and composition, forming
   384                                  ; the basis for arithmetic, logic, and control flow in Forth.
   385                                  
   386                                  ; -----------------------
   387                                  ; -- Assembly Note - rot
   388                                  ; -----------------------
   389                                  ; Implements rot in straight x86 assembly: pops two values into
   390                                  ; registers, pushes them back in rotated order, then swaps AX/BX.
   391                                  ; Ends with jmp next to continue inner interpreter dispatch.
   392                                  
   393                                  ; -----------------------
   394                                  ; -- Forth Note - rot
   395                                  ; -----------------------
   396                                  ; `rot` rearranges the top three items of the parameter stack. In
   397                                  ; Forth code, it allows manipulation of stack order for arithmetic
   398                                  ; or data access.
   399                                  
   400                                                                          ; rot
   401 0000008B [7E00]                  lfa_rot:            dw lfa_lit
   402 0000008D 03726F74                nfa_rot:            db 3,'rot'
   403 00000091 [9300]                  cfa_rot:            dw $+2
   404                                  
   405 00000093 5A                                          pop dx
   406 00000094 58                                          pop ax
   407 00000095 52                                          push dx
   408 00000096 53                                          push bx
   409 00000097 93                                          xchg ax,bx
   410 00000098 EB55                                        jmp next
   411                                  
   412                                  ; -----------------------
   413                                  ; -- Assembly Note - drop
   414                                  ; -----------------------
   415                                  ; Implements `drop` by popping the top item off the parameter stack
   416                                  ; into BX, effectively discarding it. Ends with `jmp next` to resume
   417                                  ; inner interpreter dispatch.
   418                                  
   419                                  ; ---------------------
   420                                  ; -- Forth Note - drop
   421                                  ; ---------------------
   422                                  ; `drop` removes the top stack item. It’s one of the most basic
   423                                  ; stack manipulation words and is used pervasively in Forth code.
   424                                  
   425                                                                          ; drop
   426 0000009A [8B00]                  lfa_drop:           dw lfa_rot
   427 0000009C 0464726F70              nfa_drop:           db 4,'drop'
   428 000000A1 [A300]                  cfa_drop:           dw $+2
   429                                  
   430 000000A3 5B                                          pop bx
   431 000000A4 EB49                                        jmp next
   432                                  
   433                                  ; -----------------------
   434                                  ; -- Assembly Note - dup
   435                                  ; -----------------------
   436                                  ; Implements `dup` by pushing BX, which holds the top of the parameter
   437                                  ; stack, creating a duplicate of the top item. Ends with `jmp next`.
   438                                  
   439                                  ; ---------------------
   440                                  ; -- Forth Note - dup
   441                                  ; ---------------------
   442                                  ; `dup` duplicates the top stack item. It’s a core stack operation used
   443                                  ; frequently in arithmetic, control structures, and data handling.
   444                                  
   445                                                                          ; dup
   446 000000A6 [9A00]                  lfa_dup:            dw lfa_drop
   447 000000A8 03647570                nfa_dup:            db 3,'dup'
   448 000000AC [AE00]                  cfa_dup:            dw $+2
   449                                  
   450 000000AE 53                                          push bx
   451 000000AF EB3E                                        jmp next
   452                                  
   453                                  ; ------------------------
   454                                  ; -- Assembly Note - swap
   455                                  ; ------------------------
   456                                  ; Implements `swap` by popping the second stack item into AX, pushing
   457                                  ; the top item (BX), then exchanging AX and BX. This flips the top
   458                                  ; two stack items. Ends with `jmp next`.
   459                                  
   460                                  ; ----------------------
   461                                  ; -- Forth Note - swap
   462                                  ; ----------------------
   463                                  ; `swap` exchanges the top two stack items. It is a fundamental
   464                                  ; stack manipulation word, often paired with `dup` and `rot`.
   465                                  
   466                                                                          ; swap
   467 000000B1 [A600]                  lfa_swap:           dw lfa_dup
   468 000000B3 0473776170              nfa_swap:           db 4,'swap'
   469 000000B8 [BA00]                  cfa_swap:           dw $+2
   470                                  
   471 000000BA 58                                          pop ax
   472 000000BB 53                                          push bx
   473 000000BC 93                                          xchg ax,bx
   474 000000BD EB30                                        jmp next
   475                                  
   476                                  ; ---------
   477                                  ; -- Dictionary Words: plus [+], equals [=]
   478                                  ; -- Math and Logic Primitives
   479                                  ; ---------
   480                                  
   481                                  ; ------------------------------
   482                                  ; -- Assembly Note - Math and Logic Primitives
   483                                  ; ------------------------------
   484                                  ; Math and logic primitives are implemented with short inlined assembly
   485                                  ; sequences that operate directly on the parameter stack using AX and BX.
   486                                  ; Arithmetic words like `+` modify BX in place, while logic words like `=`
   487                                  ; perform comparisons and set BX to true (-1) or false (0). Each ends with
   488                                  ; `jmp next` to return to the inner interpreter.
   489                                  
   490                                  ; ----------------------------
   491                                  ; -- Forth Note - Math and Logic Primitives
   492                                  ; ----------------------------
   493                                  ; These primitives provide the core arithmetic and comparison operations
   494                                  ; needed by Forth programs. They manipulate values on the parameter stack
   495                                  ; and return results in the standard Forth fashion, supporting control
   496                                  ; structures, calculations, and decision-making.
   497                                  
   498                                  ; ------------------------
   499                                  ; -- Assembly Note - plus
   500                                  ; ------------------------
   501                                  ; Implements `+` by popping the second stack item into AX, adding it
   502                                  ; to BX (top of stack), and leaving the result in BX. Ends with
   503                                  ; `jmp next` to resume interpretation.
   504                                  
   505                                  ; ----------------------
   506                                  ; -- Forth Note - plus
   507                                  ; ----------------------
   508                                  ; `+` adds the top two stack items and pushes the result. It’s a core
   509                                  ; arithmetic primitive used throughout Forth code.
   510                                  
   511                                                                          ; plus [+]
   512 000000BF [B100]                  lfa_plus:           dw lfa_swap
   513 000000C1 012B                    nfa_plus:           db 1,'+'
   514 000000C3 [C500]                  cfa_plus:           dw $+2
   515                                  
   516 000000C5 58                                          pop ax
   517 000000C6 01C3                                        add bx,ax
   518 000000C8 EB25                                        jmp next
   519                                  
   520                                  ; --------------------------
   521                                  ; -- Assembly Note - equals
   522                                  ; --------------------------
   523                                  ; Implements `=` by popping the second stack item into AX and comparing
   524                                  ; it with BX. Sets BX to true (-1) if equal, or false (0) if not. Ends
   525                                  ; with `jmp next`.
   526                                  
   527                                  ; ------------------------
   528                                  ; -- Forth Note - equals
   529                                  ; ------------------------
   530                                  ; `=` compares the top two stack items and pushes a true flag (-1) if
   531                                  ; they are equal, or false (0) otherwise. This is a basic logical
   532                                  ; primitive used in conditionals and branching.
   533                                  
   534                                                                          ; equals [=]
   535 000000CA [BF00]                  lfa_equals:         dw lfa_plus
   536 000000CC 013D                    nfa_equals:         db 1,'='
   537 000000CE [D000]                  cfa_equals:         dw $+2
   538                                  
   539 000000D0 58                                          pop ax
   540 000000D1 29C3                                        sub bx,ax
   541 000000D3 83EB01                                      sub bx,1
   542 000000D6 19DB                                        sbb bx,bx
   543 000000D8 EB15                                        jmp next
   544                                  
   545                                  ; ---------
   546                                  ; -- Dictionary Words: fetch [@], store [!]
   547                                  ; -- Memory Access Primitives
   548                                  ; ---------
   549                                  
   550                                  ; -----------------------
   551                                  ; -- Assembly Note - Memory Access Primitives
   552                                  ; -----------------------
   553                                  ; These primitives implement direct memory fetch and store using the
   554                                  ; address on the parameter stack. `fetch` loads the 16-bit value at
   555                                  ; the address in BX into BX. `store` writes the top of stack to the
   556                                  ; address in BX, then pops the address itself. Both end with `jmp next`.
   557                                  
   558                                  ; -----------------------
   559                                  ; -- Forth Note - Memory Access Primitives
   560                                  ; -----------------------
   561                                  ; `@` and `!` provide the fundamental mechanism for reading and writing
   562                                  ; memory in Forth. `@` replaces the top stack item (an address) with the
   563                                  ; contents of that address. `!` takes a value and an address from the stack
   564                                  ; and stores the value into that address. They are low-level building blocks
   565                                  ; for variables, constants, and user data structures.
   566                                  
   567                                  ; -----------------------
   568                                  ; -- Assembly Note - fetch [@]
   569                                  ; -----------------------
   570                                  ; Loads the 16-bit word at [BX] into BX. This replaces the address on top
   571                                  ; of the stack with the fetched value. Ends with `jmp next`.
   572                                  
   573                                  ; -----------------------
   574                                  ; -- Forth Note - fetch [@]
   575                                  ; -----------------------
   576                                  ; Takes an address from the stack and replaces it with the contents of
   577                                  ; that address.
   578                                  
   579                                                                          ; fetch [!]
   580 000000DA [CA00]                  lfa_fetch:          dw lfa_equals
   581 000000DC 0140                    nfa_fetch:          db 1,'@'
   582 000000DE [E000]                  cfa_fetch:          dw $+2
   583                                  
   584 000000E0 8B1F                                        mov bx,word[bx]
   585 000000E2 EB0B                                        jmp next
   586                                  
   587                                  ; -----------------------
   588                                  ; -- Assembly Note - store [!]
   589                                  ; -----------------------
   590                                  ; Pops the top of stack into [BX], then pops the address itself into BX.
   591                                  ; This stores the value at the specified address, then discards the address.
   592                                  ; Ends with `jmp next`.
   593                                  
   594                                  ; -----------------------
   595                                  ; -- Forth Note - store [!]
   596                                  ; -----------------------
   597                                  ; Takes a value and an address from the stack and stores the value at
   598                                  ; the address. The address and value are both consumed.
   599                                  
   600                                                                          ; store [!]
   601 000000E4 [DA00]                  lfa_store:          dw lfa_fetch
   602 000000E6 0121                    nfa_store:          db 1,'!'
   603 000000E8 [EA00]                  cfa_store:          dw $+2
   604                                  
   605 000000EA 8F07                                        pop word[bx]
   606 000000EC 5B                                          pop bx
   607 000000ED EB00                                        jmp next
   608                                  
   609                                  ; ------------------------------
   610                                  ; -- Forth Note - Word Chain after Core Primitives
   611                                  ; ------------------------------
   612                                  ; The dictionary now includes the head, core variables, and the
   613                                  ; foundational primitives required to bootstrap the inner interpreter:
   614                                  ;
   615                                  ;   0 <- state <- to_in [>in] <- ntib [#tib] <- dp <- base <- last <- tib
   616                                  ;
   617                                  ; Each entry links to the previous one, forming a chain that supports
   618                                  ; early lookup and compilation. This setup allows the inner interpreter
   619                                  ; to begin executing once defined and provides the minimal environment
   620                                  ; for primitive operations.
   621                                  
   622                                  ; -------------------------------------
   623                                  ; -- Inner Interpreter (next)
   624                                  ; -- Forth Fetch-and-Execute Loop
   625                                  ; -------------------------------------
   626                                  
   627                                  ; --------------------------------------------------------------------
   628                                  ; This is the classic indirect threaded Forth inner interpreter, called “next.”
   629                                  ; Each Forth word’s definition consists of a list of code field addresses (cfa).
   630                                  ; next repeatedly fetches the next cfa and executes the code it points to.
   631                                  ;
   632                                  ; Each word’s code executes, then typically returns to next to process the next cfa.
   633                                  ; This dispatch loop is the core of the Forth execution model.
   634                                  ;
   635                                  ; Startup behavior:
   636                                  ;   si is initialized to cfa_interpret+2 and execution jumps to next.
   637                                  ;   This skips fetching interpret’s own cfa.
   638                                  ;   The first lodsw fetches the cfa of the first word in interpret’s thread
   639                                  ;   and jumps to it, entering the interpreter loop directly.
   640                                  ;
   641                                  ; The next routine appears here among the word definitions because it is
   642                                  ; part of the runtime environment and is referenced like any other word.
   643                                  ; Placing it here allows references to its address to be resolved during the build
   644                                  ; and ensures it participates in the threaded code sequence.
   645                                  ; --------------------------------------------------------------------
   646                                  
   647 000000EF AD                      next:               lodsw               ; load word at [si] into ax, increment si by 2
   648 000000F0 97                                          xchg di,ax          ; di now holds the fetched cfa
   649 000000F1 FF25                                        jmp word[di]        ; indirect jump to the cfa
   650                                  
   651                                  ; -------------------------------------
   652                                  ; -- Interpreter-Dependent Primitives
   653                                  ; -- Require the inner interpreter to execute via cfa
   654                                  ; -------------------------------------
   655                                  
   656                                  ; ------------------------------
   657                                  ; -- Assembly Note - Interpreter-Dependent Primitives
   658                                  ; ------------------------------
   659                                  ; These primitives are implemented as standard threaded-code routines that
   660                                  ; assume the inner interpreter is active. They use indirect jumps through
   661                                  ; code field addresses to transfer control, relying on the interpreter’s
   662                                  ; fetch-and-dispatch loop rather than executing standalone machine code.
   663                                  
   664                                  ; ----------------------------
   665                                  ; -- Forth Note - Interpreter-Dependent Primitives
   666                                  ; ----------------------------
   667                                  ; These words form part of the core runtime and require the inner interpreter
   668                                  ; to be running. They rely on `next` to fetch and execute the code field
   669                                  ; addresses of subsequent words, integrating tightly with the interpreter’s
   670                                  ; control flow.
   671                                  
   672                                  
   673                                  ; ---------
   674                                  ; -- Dictionary words: zero_branch [0branch], branch, execute, exit
   675                                  ; -- Colon Flow Control Primitives
   676                                  ; ---------
   677                                  
   678                                  ; ------------------------------
   679                                  ; -- Assembly Note - Colon Flow Control Primitives
   680                                  ; ------------------------------
   681                                  ; These primitives manage control flow within colon definitions. `0branch`
   682                                  ; conditionally skips ahead by a 16-bit offset if the top of stack is zero.
   683                                  ; `branch` always jumps ahead by the given offset. `execute` transfers control
   684                                  ; to the cfa on the stack, and `exit` returns to the caller by restoring the
   685                                  ; interpreter’s instruction pointer. Each uses direct manipulation of SI and
   686                                  ; indirect jumps to integrate with the threaded execution model.
   687                                  
   688                                  ; ----------------------------
   689                                  ; -- Forth Note - Colon Flow Control Primitives
   690                                  ; ----------------------------
   691                                  ; These primitives implement the basic control structures used in colon
   692                                  ; definitions. `0branch` and `branch` handle conditional and unconditional
   693                                  ; jumps within a thread. `execute` allows computed transfers of control,
   694                                  ; and `exit` returns from a colon definition. Together they enable looping,
   695                                  ; branching, and dynamic execution flow in Forth programs.
   696                                  
   697                                  ; ------------------------------
   698                                  ; -- Assembly Note - zero_branch
   699                                  ; ------------------------------
   700                                  ; Fetches a 16-bit offset from the thread, tests BX, and if zero updates SI.
   701                                  ; Otherwise continues execution. Ends with `jmp next` to return to the inner interpreter.
   702                                  
   703                                  ; ----------------------------
   704                                  ; -- Forth Note - zero_branch
   705                                  ; ----------------------------
   706                                  ; Implements conditional branching in colon definitions. Skips the following code
   707                                  ; if the top of stack is false (0), supporting if-then and loop constructs.
   708                                  
   709                                                                          ; zero_branch [0branch]
   710 000000F3 [E400]                  lfa_zero_branch:    dw lfa_store
   711 000000F5 07306272616E6368        nfa_zero_branch:    db 7,'0branch'
   712 000000FD [FF00]                  cfa_zero_branch:    dw $+2
   713                                  
   714 000000FF AD                                          lodsw
   715 00000100 85DB                                        test bx,bx
   716 00000102 7501                                        jne zerob_z
   717 00000104 96                                          xchg ax,si
   718 00000105 5B                      zerob_z:            pop bx
   719 00000106 EBE7                                        jmp next
   720                                  
   721                                  ; ------------------------------
   722                                  ; -- Assembly Note - branch
   723                                  ; ------------------------------
   724                                  ; Loads a 16-bit offset from the thread and updates SI unconditionally.
   725                                  ; Ends with `jmp next` to continue inner interpreter dispatch.
   726                                  
   727                                  ; ----------------------------
   728                                  ; -- Forth Note - branch
   729                                  ; ----------------------------
   730                                  ; Implements unconditional branching in colon definitions, used for loops and jumps.
   731                                  
   732                                                                          ; branch
   733 00000108 [F300]                  lfa_branch:         dw lfa_zero_branch
   734 0000010A 066272616E6368          nfa_branch:         db 6,'branch'
   735 00000111 [1301]                  cfa_branch:         dw $+2
   736                                  
   737 00000113 8B34                                        mov si,word[si]
   738 00000115 EBD8                                        jmp next
   739                                  
   740                                  ; ------------------------------
   741                                  ; -- Assembly Note - execute
   742                                  ; ------------------------------
   743                                  ; Loads the CFA from the top of stack (BX) into DI, pops BX, and jumps to that CFA.
   744                                  ; This allows one word to invoke another dynamically at runtime.
   745                                  
   746                                  ; ----------------------------
   747                                  ; -- Forth Note - execute
   748                                  ; ----------------------------
   749                                  ; Performs indirect execution of any Forth word. Supports higher-order programming
   750                                  ; and dynamic execution within colon definitions.
   751                                  
   752                                  
   753                                                                          ; execute
   754 00000117 [0801]                  lfa_execute:        dw lfa_branch
   755 00000119 0765786563757465        nfa_execute:        db 7,'execute'
   756 00000121 [2301]                  cfa_execute:        dw $+2
   757                                  
   758 00000123 89DF                                        mov di,bx
   759 00000125 5B                                          pop bx
   760 00000126 FF25                                        jmp word[di]
   761                                  
   762                                  ; ------------------------------
   763                                  ; -- Assembly Note - exit
   764                                  ; ------------------------------
   765                                  ; Restores SI from the return stack via BP, increments BP twice, and jumps to `next`.
   766                                  ; Returns control from a colon definition back to the calling context.
   767                                  
   768                                  ; ----------------------------
   769                                  ; -- Forth Note - exit
   770                                  ; ----------------------------
   771                                  ; Ends execution of a colon definition and returns to the calling word.
   772                                  ; Essential for structured control flow in Forth programs.
   773                                  
   774                                                                          ; exit
   775 00000128 [1701]                  lfa_exit:           dw lfa_execute
   776 0000012A 0465786974              nfa_exit:           db 4,'exit'
   777 0000012F [3101]                  cfa_exit:           dw $+2
   778                                  
   779 00000131 8B7600                                      mov si,word[bp]
   780 00000134 45                                          inc bp
   781 00000135 45                                          inc bp
   782 00000136 EBB7                                        jmp next
   783                                  
   784                                  ; ---------
   785                                  ; -- Dictionary words: count, to_number [>number]
   786                                  ; -- String Primitives
   787                                  ; ---------
   788                                  
   789                                  ; ------------------------------
   790                                  ; -- Assembly Note - String Primitives
   791                                  ; ------------------------------
   792                                  ; String primitives manipulate memory addresses and counters to handle
   793                                  ; Forth strings. `count` loads the length-prefixed string and pushes its
   794                                  ; address and length. `>number` converts character sequences into numeric
   795                                  ; values, updating the parameter stack. Each ends with `jmp next`.
   796                                  
   797                                  ; ----------------------------
   798                                  ; -- Forth Note - String Primitives
   799                                  ; ----------------------------
   800                                  ; These primitives provide basic string handling: `count` accesses strings
   801                                  ; by address and length, while `>number` parses numeric input from text,
   802                                  ; supporting input conversion and numeric operations in Forth programs.
   803                                  
   804                                  ; -----------------------
   805                                  ; -- Assembly Note - count
   806                                  ; -----------------------
   807                                  ; `count` increments BX, pushes it on the stack, and loads the string
   808                                  ; length into BL/BH registers. Ends with `jmp next` to continue execution.
   809                                  
   810                                  ; -----------------------
   811                                  ; -- Forth Note - count
   812                                  ; -----------------------
   813                                  ; Provides the address and length of a counted string. Used for reading
   814                                  ; words or tokens from the input buffer for further processing.
   815                                  
   816                                                                          ; count
   817 00000138 [2801]                  lfa_count:          dw lfa_exit
   818 0000013A 05636F756E74            nfa_count:          db 5,'count'
   819 00000140 [4201]                  cfa_count:          dw $+2
   820                                  
   821 00000142 43                                          inc bx
   822 00000143 53                                          push bx
   823 00000144 8A5FFF                                      mov bl,byte[bx-1]
   824 00000147 B700                                        mov bh,0
   825 00000149 EBA4                                        jmp next
   826                                  
   827                                  ; -----------------------
   828                                  ; -- Assembly Note - >number
   829                                  ; -----------------------
   830                                  ; `>number` converts a sequence of characters at DI into a numeric value,
   831                                  ; updating AX/CX/DX and the parameter stack. Implements numeric parsing
   832                                  ; directly in assembly. Ends with `jmp next`.
   833                                  
   834                                  ; -----------------------
   835                                  ; -- Forth Note - >number
   836                                  ; -----------------------
   837                                  ; Converts a string of digits into a number and pushes it on the stack.
   838                                  ; Supports decimal input and integrates with standard Forth numeric words.
   839                                  
   840                                                                          ; to_number [>number]
   841 0000014B [3801]                  lfa_to_number:      dw lfa_count
   842 0000014D 073E6E756D626572        nfa_to_number:      db 7,'>number'
   843 00000155 [5701]                  cfa_to_number:      dw $+2
   844                                  
   845 00000157 5F                                          pop di
   846 00000158 59                                          pop cx
   847 00000159 58                                          pop ax
   848 0000015A 85DB                    to_numl:            test bx,bx
   849 0000015C 7433                                        je to_numz
   850 0000015E 50                                          push ax
   851 0000015F 8A05                                        mov al,byte[di]
   852 00000161 3C61                                        cmp al,'a'
   853 00000163 7202                                        jc to_nums
   854 00000165 2C20                                        sub al,32
   855 00000167 3C3A                    to_nums:            cmp al,'9'+1
   856 00000169 7206                                        jc to_numg
   857 0000016B 3C41                                        cmp al,'A'
   858 0000016D 7223                                        jc to_numh
   859 0000016F 2C07                                        sub al,7
   860 00000171 2C30                    to_numg:            sub al,48
   861 00000173 B400                                        mov ah,0
   862 00000175 3A06[3500]                                  cmp al,byte[dfa_base]
   863 00000179 7317                                        jnc to_numh
   864 0000017B 92                                          xchg ax,dx
   865 0000017C 58                                          pop ax
   866 0000017D 52                                          push dx
   867 0000017E 91                                          xchg ax,cx
   868 0000017F F726[3500]                                  mul word[dfa_base]
   869 00000183 91                                          xchg ax,cx
   870 00000184 F726[3500]                                  mul word[dfa_base]
   871 00000188 01D1                                        add cx,dx
   872 0000018A 5A                                          pop dx
   873 0000018B 01D0                                        add ax,dx
   874 0000018D 4B                                          dec bx
   875 0000018E 47                                          inc di
   876 0000018F EBC9                                        jmp to_numl
   877 00000191 50                      to_numz:            push ax
   878 00000192 51                      to_numh:            push cx
   879 00000193 57                                          push di
   880 00000194 E958FF                                      jmp next
   881                                  
   882                                  ; ---------
   883                                  ; -- Dictionary words: accept, word, emit
   884                                  ; -- Terminal I/O Primitives
   885                                  ; -----------------------
   886                                  
   887                                  ; ------------------------------
   888                                  ; -- Assembly Note - Terminal I/O Primitives
   889                                  ; ------------------------------
   890                                  ; The terminal I/O primitives are implemented in straight x86 assembly.
   891                                  ; `accept` reads a line of characters from input, handles backspaces,
   892                                  ; updates the buffer pointer (>in) and character count (#tib), and echoes
   893                                  ; characters to the terminal. `word` extracts a token from the input buffer,
   894                                  ; copying it to dictionary space and terminating with a space. `emit` writes
   895                                  ; a character from the stack to the terminal via `outchar`. Each primitive
   896                                  ; ends with `jmp next` to continue the inner interpreter loop.
   897                                  
   898                                  ; ------------------------------
   899                                  ; -- Forth Note - Terminal I/O Primitives
   900                                  ; ------------------------------
   901                                  ; These primitives provide the Forth environment with basic interaction
   902                                  ; facilities. `accept` allows user input for programs, `word` tokenizes
   903                                  ; input for interpretation or compilation, and `emit` outputs characters.
   904                                  ; Together, they enable reading, parsing, and writing text in Forth programs,
   905                                  ; forming the foundation for interactive use.
   906                                  
   907                                  ; ------------------------------
   908                                  ; -- Assembly Note - accept
   909                                  ; ------------------------------
   910                                  ; Implements the `accept` primitive in straight x86 assembly. It reads a
   911                                  ; line of input from the user, handles backspace, updates the input buffer
   912                                  ; pointer (>in) and character count (#tib), and echoes typed characters.
   913                                  ; Control characters like carriage return and bell are processed correctly.
   914                                  ; Ends with `jmp next` to return to the inner interpreter loop.
   915                                  
   916                                  ; ------------------------------
   917                                  ; -- Forth Note - accept
   918                                  ; ------------------------------
   919                                  ; `accept` reads a line of text into the input buffer for further processing.
   920                                  ; It updates the buffer pointer (>in) and character count (#tib). Programs
   921                                  ; and the outer interpreter rely on it to fetch user input for interpretation
   922                                  ; or compilation.
   923                                  
   924                                                                          ; accept
   925 00000197 [4B01]                  lfa_accept:         dw lfa_to_number
   926 00000199 06616363657074          nfa_accept:         db 6,'accept'
   927 000001A0 [A201]                  cfa_accept:         dw $+2
   928                                  
   929 000001A2 5F                                          pop di
   930 000001A3 31C9                                        xor cx,cx
   931 000001A5 E89E00                  acceptl:            call getchar
   932 000001A8 3C08                                        cmp al,8
   933 000001AA 7513                                        jne acceptn
   934 000001AC E319                                        jcxz acceptb
   935 000001AE E89C00                                      call outchar
   936 000001B1 B020                                        mov al,' '
   937 000001B3 E89700                                      call outchar
   938 000001B6 B008                                        mov al,8
   939 000001B8 E89200                                      call outchar
   940 000001BB 49                                          dec cx
   941 000001BC 4F                                          dec di
   942 000001BD EBE6                                        jmp acceptl
   943 000001BF 3C0D                    acceptn:            cmp al,13
   944 000001C1 7412                                        je acceptz
   945 000001C3 39D9                                        cmp cx,bx
   946 000001C5 7507                                        jne accepts
   947 000001C7 B007                    acceptb:            mov al,7
   948 000001C9 E88100                                      call outchar
   949 000001CC EBD7                                        jmp acceptl
   950 000001CE AA                      accepts:            stosb
   951 000001CF 41                                          inc cx
   952 000001D0 E87A00                                      call outchar
   953 000001D3 EBD0                                        jmp acceptl
   954 000001D5 E3F0                    acceptz:            jcxz acceptb
   955 000001D7 B00D                                        mov al,13
   956 000001D9 E87100                                      call outchar
   957 000001DC B00A                                        mov al,10
   958 000001DE E86C00                                      call outchar
   959 000001E1 89CB                                        mov bx,cx
   960 000001E3 E909FF                                      jmp next
   961                                  
   962                                  ; ------------------------------
   963                                  ; -- Assembly Note - word
   964                                  ; ------------------------------
   965                                  ; `word` is implemented entirely in x86 assembly. It scans the input
   966                                  ; buffer starting at `>in` in the terminal input area, copies characters
   967                                  ; up to a delimiter into the dictionary at `dp`, and updates `>in` to
   968                                  ; point past the consumed input. Ends with `jmp next` to return control
   969                                  ; to the inner interpreter.
   970                                  
   971                                  ; ------------------------------
   972                                  ; -- Forth Note - word
   973                                  ; ------------------------------
   974                                  ; This primitive reads the next space-delimited token from the input
   975                                  ; buffer. Forth programs use it to parse words for interpretation or
   976                                  ; compilation. It handles buffer management and updates `>in`, making
   977                                  ; subsequent primitives aware of the current input position.
   978                                  
   979                                                                          ; word
   980 000001E6 [9701]                  lfa_word:           dw lfa_accept
   981 000001E8 04776F7264              nfa_word:           db 4,'word'
   982 000001ED [EF01]                  cfa_word:           dw $+2
   983                                  
   984 000001EF 8B3E[2A00]                                  mov di,word[dfa_dp]
   985 000001F3 57                                          push di
   986 000001F4 89DA                                        mov dx,bx
   987 000001F6 8B1E[4A00]                                  mov bx,word[dfa_tib]
   988 000001FA 89D9                                        mov cx,bx
   989 000001FC 031E[1600]                                  add bx,word[dfa_to_in]
   990 00000200 030E[2100]                                  add cx,word[dfa_ntib]
   991 00000204 39D9                    wordf:              cmp cx,bx
   992 00000206 7415                                        je wordz
   993 00000208 8A07                                        mov al,byte[bx]
   994 0000020A 43                                          inc bx
   995 0000020B 38D0                                        cmp al,dl
   996 0000020D 74F5                                        je wordf
   997 0000020F 47                      wordc:              inc di
   998 00000210 8805                                        mov byte[di],al
   999 00000212 39D9                                        cmp cx,bx
  1000 00000214 7407                                        je wordz
  1001 00000216 8A07                                        mov al,byte[bx]
  1002 00000218 43                                          inc bx
  1003 00000219 38D0                                        cmp al,dl
  1004 0000021B 75F2                                        jne wordc
  1005 0000021D C6450120                wordz:              mov byte[di+1],32
  1006 00000221 A1[2A00]                                    mov ax,word[dfa_dp]
  1007 00000224 97                                          xchg ax,di
  1008 00000225 29F8                                        sub ax,di
  1009 00000227 8805                                        mov byte[di],al
  1010 00000229 2B1E[4A00]                                  sub bx,word[dfa_tib]
  1011 0000022D 891E[1600]                                  mov word[dfa_to_in],bx
  1012 00000231 5B                                          pop bx
  1013 00000232 E9BAFE                                      jmp next
  1014                                  
  1015                                  ; ------------------------------
  1016                                  ; -- Assembly Note - emit
  1017                                  ; ------------------------------
  1018                                  ; `emit` swaps AX/BX to place the character in AL, then calls DOS
  1019                                  ; interrupt 21h function 2 to write the character to the standard
  1020                                  ; output. Returns to the inner interpreter with `jmp next`. Uses
  1021                                  ; `getchar` and `outchar` as low-level I/O helpers.
  1022                                  
  1023                                  ; ------------------------------
  1024                                  ; -- Forth Note - emit
  1025                                  ; ------------------------------
  1026                                  ; Outputs a single character from the parameter stack to the
  1027                                  ; terminal. Forth programs call `emit` to display characters or
  1028                                  ; produce text output; it operates on the top of the parameter stack.
  1029                                  
  1030                                                                          ; emit
  1031 00000235 [E601]                  lfa_emit:           dw lfa_word
  1032 00000237 04656D6974              nfa_emit:           db 4,'emit'
  1033 0000023C [3E02]                  cfa_emit:           dw $+2
  1034                                  
  1035 0000023E 93                                          xchg ax,bx
  1036 0000023F E80B00                                      call outchar
  1037 00000242 5B                                          pop bx
  1038 00000243 E9A9FE                                      jmp next
  1039                                  
  1040 00000246 B407                    getchar:            mov ah,7
  1041 00000248 CD21                                        int 021h
  1042 0000024A B400                                        mov ah,0
  1043 0000024C C3                                          ret
  1044                                  
  1045 0000024D 92                      outchar:            xchg ax,dx
  1046 0000024E B402                                        mov ah,2
  1047 00000250 CD21                                        int 021h
  1048 00000252 C3                                          ret
  1049                                  
  1050                                  ; ---------
  1051                                  ; -- Dictionary word: find
  1052                                  ; -- Search Primitive
  1053                                  ; -----------------------
  1054                                  
  1055                                  ; ------------------------------
  1056                                  ; -- Assembly Note - find
  1057                                  ; ------------------------------
  1058                                  ; Implements dictionary search for a given name. Traverses linked
  1059                                  ; headers starting at last, compares characters case-insensitively,
  1060                                  ; sets BX to indicate success (1) or failure (0). On success, DI points
  1061                                  ; to the word’s CFA. Returns to next for further execution.
  1062                                  
  1063                                  ; ------------------------------
  1064                                  ; -- Forth Note - find
  1065                                  ; ------------------------------
  1066                                  ; `find` is a Forth word that searches the dictionary for a given name.
  1067                                  ; When called by the outer interpreter, it determines whether input
  1068                                  ; matches an existing definition. On success, the word can be executed
  1069                                  ; or compiled; on failure, the search result signals undefined input.
  1070                                  
  1071                                                                          ; find
  1072 00000253 [3502]                  lfa_find:           dw lfa_emit
  1073 00000255 0466696E64              nfa_find:           db 4,'find'
  1074 0000025A [5C02]                  cfa_find:           dw $+2
  1075                                  
  1076 0000025C BF[4000]                                    mov di,dfa_last
  1077 0000025F 57                      findl:              push di
  1078 00000260 53                                          push bx
